    "slug": "modes-two-families",
    "title": "Two Homes, Two Dials Each",
    "summary": "The ladder splits into a major-home family and a minor-home family at the point where the 3 flattens — so there are not seven scales to learn, there are two homes with two dials each.",
    "tags": ["modes", "theory"],
    "readingTimeMin": 3,
    "publishedAt": "2026-08-15"
  },
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "One of those six one-note steps changes more than a colour. Mixolydian to Dorian is where the "
        },
        {
          "text": "3",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " flattens, and the "
        },
        {
          "text": "3",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " is the note that decides whether the chord under your hand is major or minor. Everything above that step on the ladder has a major home chord; everything below it has a minor one, until you reach Locrian."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Above the split: Lydian, Ionian, Mixolydian, all with "
        },
        {
          "text": "A",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " for a home chord — "
        },
        {
          "text": "A C# E",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", "
        },
        {
          "text": "1 3 5",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". Below it: Dorian, Aeolian, Phrygian, all with "
        },
        {
          "text": "Am",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " — "
        },
        {
          "text": "A C E",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", "
        },
        {
          "text": "1 b3 5",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Locrian sits outside both, because its fifth flattens too: its home chord is "
        },
        {
          "text": "Adim",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", "
        },
        {
          "text": "A C Eb",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", "
        },
        {
          "text": "1 b3 b5",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". Whether a chord with no perfect fifth can actually hold down a home is a question a later chapter takes up; for now, just the chord."
        }
      ]
    },
