    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Which turns the whole roster into something much smaller: two homes, two dials each. In the major family, the dials are the "
        },
        {
          "text": "4",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and the "
        },
        {
          "text": "7",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " — Lydian raises the first, Ionian sits at the reference setting, Mixolydian lowers the second. In the minor family, the dials are the "
        },
        {
          "text": "6",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and the "
        },
        {
          "text": "2",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " — Dorian raises the first, Aeolian sits at the reference setting, Phrygian lowers the second. Turn one dial and you move exactly one rung on the ladder. Seven scales on "
        },
        {
          "text": "A",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " collapse into two chords and four switches — which is the whole reason this pathway stays on one root the whole way through."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Ionian and Aeolian sit at the middle setting of their own family — the reference each family is heard against, which is exactly why neither one carries a characteristic note. There's nothing to point at that isn't also true of the reference itself."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Chapter 3 takes the top row, one mode at a time; chapter 4 takes the bottom row the same way. Neither happens here — this lesson is the map, not the tour."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Hear the two homes back to back."
        }
      ]
    },
